    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nPorsche Mission S: This Is What the New Hypercar Concept Will Look Like – Rendering\n11 photos\nPhoto: Instagram | avarvarii\nAfter a long wait, Porsche is finally ready to launch a new hypercar, and it being previewed by the Mission S Concept that will be unveiled a few hours from now.\nFollowing in the footsteps of the iconic Porsche 918 Spyder, the Mission S recently leaked in all its glory, and that short video has been used by @avarvarii to create a set of very realistic renderings. The digital illustrations shared above reveal how the upcoming model will look, and we have to admit that it is unlike anything we have seen from the Zuffenhausen brand, at least when it comes to the styling of the back end.\nIn fact, these CGIs focus solely on the car’s rear end, as it spilled the beans on its design from this angle. We can see that the fat rear three-quarter panels blend in with the bumper and diffuser on the lower part of the body, and with the integrated wing on the deck. The taillights are very slim, and “ Mission ” emblem sits between them. Further down, we can see a rather discreet diffuser, and cutouts for the vertically stacked quad exhaust tips.\nIt also features rear glass panels similar to those of the Bugatti Chiron, to some extent, obviously, and we can also see that the engine cover has a fan mounted on top of it. And that sends Porsche 917 vibes. The Mission S Concept has a vented hood at the front, extremely muscular front fenders, sharp-looking side skirts, traditional door mirrors, and it looks ready to give other supercars a headache. Let’s just hope that the final product looks this cool.\nPhoto: Instagram | avarvarii Previous reports indicated that the Mission S might draw some inspiration from the Porsche Mission X Concept unveiled in 2023, but from what we can tell, it does not have anything in common with that show car. The new study is part of the company’s 35 plan detailed recently by the company’s head honcho, Michael Leiters. And in case you missed it, you should know that the automaker is struggling financially, and believes that making fewer cars and scoring a huge profit on each one is the key to success.\nThat is where the Porsche Mission S comes in, as it gives us a first look at “ a potential mid-engine super sports car platform for the future. ” That is pretty much everything we know about it so far. Well, that and the design of the back end that has recently leaked in all its glory, hours before the model’s official premiere. Nevertheless, it is logical to assume that the hypercar will sit well above the 911 series, which costs upward of $135,500 for the base Carrera and reaches almost $300,000 when it comes to the Turbo S .\nIt is safe to say that the production model will be aimed at enthusiasts with very deep pockets. Reports indicate that it will be priced at well over half a million dollars, though some believe that it could end up costing as much as $1.5 million. Or maybe even more. And the model won’t only follow in the footsteps of the 918 Spyder, but also the Carrera GT.\nThe latter model was made in 1,270 examples at the Leipzig factory in Germany between 2003 and 2006. It was a two-door Targa roadster with a longitudinally mounted rear mid-engine and rear-wheel drive layout. Power came from a 5.7-liter V10. The mill was good for 603 horsepower (612 0s/450 kW) and 435 pound-feet (590 Nm) of torque, channeled to the wheels through a six-speed manual transmission. It dealt with 0-62 mph (0-100 kph) in 3.9 seconds, and maxed out at 205 mph (330 kph).\nPhoto: Instagram | avarvarii Its successor, the Porsche 918 Spyder, was assembled at the Zuffenhausen facility in Stuttgart, Germany, between 2013 and 2015. As its name implies, it is an open-top model (Targa), and it boasts electrified firepower. Two electric motors mounted on the front and rear axles assist the 4.6-liter V8 engine, kicking out a total system output of 875 hp (887 ps/652 kW) and 944 pound-feet (1,280 Nm) of torque.\nTaking on the likes of the McLaren P1 and the Ferrari LaFerrari, the Porsche 918 hit 62 mph (100 kph) in as little as 2.6 seconds with the Weissach Package installed, and could keep pushing all the way up to 214 mph (345 kph).\nUsually, a Porsche 918 Spyder will set you back between over $2 million and more than $4 million in today’s used car market in the United States of America, and it is worth noting that one recently sold for more than $6 million. As for its predecessor, the Carrera GT, it tends to change hands from $1.5 million to $4.5 million, though in rare cases, this model can fetch well over $6 million.\nSo, are you excited about Porsche launching a successor to the Carrera GT and the 918? Do you like the way the Mission S looks? Tell us all about it in the comments area below. View this post on Instagram A post shared by Avarvarii Automotive Artworks (@avarvarii)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPorsche porsche concepts concepts Porsche Mission S hypercar rendering renderings\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT Apple Maps Gets DCE Update As There's Basically No Reason To Dump Google Maps",
